        {
          "id": 1176614120,
          "type": "DiffNote",
          "body": "No, it's supposed to be a length value of the first word. \n\nIf it's in the letter range, then it could be the first character of a DNS name, and it's probably too large to be a length, so treat it as an ASCII string.\n\nIf it's less than the letter range, assume it's a length and everything is formatted like RFC 1035, treat it as ENC_APN_STR as normal. (The previous cutoff was hex 20, the end of the C0 codes, but labels must start with letters, not numbers or punctuation.)\n\nIf it's outside the ASCII range, then it's bogus because the 3GPP APNs don't support pointers and compression, but add it as an ASCII string for debugging.",
          "author": {
            "id": 6764551,
            "username": "johnthacker",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "John Thacker",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
            "avatar_url": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/99c30adacedf45514aaf711ba8a44f56322ebb53e9b550b7e3c9cd83bd6a1408?s=80&d=identicon",
